  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Bilingual Receipt",
    "overview": "Every screen in Verbo is a receipt said twice: once in the reader's language and once in English, with the checked value standing still beside both. The words move between scripts (Devanagari, Telugu, Arabic, Latin); the name and the amount do not. The visual system exists to make that difference obvious: language is set large and human, money is set in fixed tabular digits, and a single letter or digit that differs is marked so it cannot be missed.\n\nThe mood is calm, exact and warm. Calm because many senders are older and unhurried, and nothing should feel urgent unless it is. Exact wherever money appears: tabular figures, the amount isolated in left-to-right order even inside Arabic, the currency written out. Warm toward both sides of a mistake, including the stranger who received money they should not have. The surfaces sit close to PayPal's account world (navy bar, white cards on pale blue paper, a single action blue) without copying it.\n\nDensity is low on the sender and recipient screens: one task per card, large type, generous space. The operator Case Desk is denser and tabular, but uses the same palette, type and corners.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Navy account bar and navy \"stage\" panels; white cards on pale blue-grey paper.",
      "One action blue for the thing to do next; green only for \"checked / safe\"; amber for \"look again\"; red for cancel and danger.",
      "Every non-English line carries an English subtitle in a smaller, quieter style beneath it.",
      "Amounts in large tabular digits; differing letters and digits marked, never just described.",
      "Pill-shaped buttons, softly rounded cards, hairline shadows."
    ],
